  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Minnesota has $1.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, with obligations down 44.1% year over year. Contracting activity produced 22,973 awards so far, with an average award size of $60.6K. The state’s obligations have moved up and down across recent fiscal years: $2.6B in FY21, $2.4B in FY22, $2.7B in FY23, $3B in FY24, $2.5B in FY25, and $1.4B in FY26. That sequence shows FY26 is materially lower than each of the prior five fiscal years. Defense and civilian agencies both play major roles: the Department of Defense accounts for $427M, while the Department of Agriculture and the Department of Veterans Affairs account for $241M and $235M respectively. Major vendors include NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORPORATION at $160M and MCGOUGH CONSTRUCTION CO., LLC at $106M. Top industry activity covers Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $161M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $125M. All figures reported are contract obligations; FY2026 totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have Minnesota's federal contract obligations changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations in FY2026 to date total $1.4B and are down 44.1% year over year. The five prior fiscal years show higher totals: $2.6B in FY21, $2.4B in FY22, $2.7B in FY23, $3B in FY24, and $2.5B in FY25. That sequence places FY26 well below the recent peak of $3B in FY24. The current-year total is a fiscal year to date figure and reflects partial-year obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries drive federal spending in Minnesota?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer at $427M, followed by the Department of Agriculture at $241M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $235M. Other notable buyers include the General Services Administration at $141M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $123M. Leading industries by obligations are Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $161M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $125M. Those sectors, plus Cheese Manufacturing at $96.5M, account for a sizeable share of FY2026 to date obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
